# Standalone Partitioned Session Handler

The server is one Go process with one HTTP ingress, a fixed partition dispatcher, and an embedded in-memory SQLite repository.

```text
POST /v1/events
  -> strict JSON decode
  -> service.NewEvent (canonical key, type, event ID, UTC time)
  -> hash full (tenant, username, canonical IP)
  -> one FIFO worker for that partition
  -> SessionService.ApplyEvent
  -> repository.Mutate transaction
  -> SQLite :memory:

POST /v1/sessions/query -> generic query preparation -> SQLite query adapter
```

## Concurrency model

There is exactly one long-lived goroutine per configured partition. Every event for the same canonical session key is routed to the same worker, so it cannot overtake an earlier event for that key. A transient failure is retried before that worker receives its next event. Other workers continue independently.

The default mutation guard is a no-op because partition ownership supplies same-key ordering. `SESSION_MUTATION_GUARD=striped` enables a fixed striped mutex fallback for same-process producers that do not preserve ownership. This guard cannot coordinate multiple processes.

SQLite deliberately retains exactly one `database/sql` connection. A bare `:memory:` database belongs to its connection, and the single connection is the embedded SQL serialization point. Partition workers may execute service code concurrently, but SQL transactions serialize at that connection.

## Data and query behavior

Sessions, states, and tags are normalized relational rows. Times are stored as signed UTC Unix nanoseconds. SQLite constraints enforce one active session per full key, one open state per active session, foreign keys, nonblank identity fields, and valid interval bounds.

Domain transitions preserve login, update, logout, duplicate, stale-event, tag-set, and half-open `[from,to)` behavior. Query preparation remains storage-neutral and owns filter normalization, fingerprints, evaluated-at snapshots, and cursor validation. SQLite cursors use storage identifier `sqlite` and keyset order `(tenant, username, IP, login time, session ID)` with binary text collation.

## Example

```text
LOGIN tenant-a / alice / 2001:0db8::1
  -> canonical key (tenant-a, alice, 2001:db8::1)
  -> FNV-1a full-key hash % partition count
  -> owning worker
  -> DecideLogin
  -> SQLite transaction: load -> insert session/state/tags -> commit
  -> HTTP 204
```

An UPDATE using `2001:db8::1` has the same canonical key and partition, so it cannot overtake the LOGIN.
